&lt;p&gt;We are hiring a Senior Engineering Manager, Cloud Infrastructure, to own and evolve the technical foundation that powers everything we build. This is a hands-on leadership role. You will be deep in the infrastructure yourself, writing Terraform, operating AKS clusters in production, and showing up to incidents with a diagnosis, not just a status update, while leading and growing a team of engineers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What You&#39;ll Do&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Own and evolve our Azure cloud infrastructure with a focus on reliability, security, and cost efficiency.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drive infrastructure-as-code practices using Terraform (and ideally Terragrunt), helping the team move toward a code-first approach to everything we build and operate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lead container operations on Kubernetes and help the team mature how we deploy and run services.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build and maintain CI/CD pipelines and DevOps practices that let engineers ship with confidence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build out and maintain our Data Infrastructure in the Cloud: Fabric, Database and Warehouses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Own incident response, from diagnosis through postmortem, and drive meaningful improvement after every significant event.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Partner with security engineering to harden infrastructure and build toward compliance goals.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build our AI-augmented observability to experiment and remediate issues faster.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lead, grow, and hire a team of infrastructure engineers — creating clarity, removing blockers, and building a culture of ownership.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Collaborate closely with Backend, Product, and Security teams on architecture decisions and major launches.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Set the standard for observability and reliability across the engineering org.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Requirements&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You have 8+ years of engineering experience, with meaningful time managing cloud infrastructure teams. You&#39;re as comfortable in a design doc or code review as you are in a leadership meeting and you operate well at both altitudes simultaneously.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You have deep, hands-on cloud infrastructure experience. Azure is our primary cloud; direct Azure experience is a plus, but strong engineers on AWS or GCP who are excited to go deep on Azure are equally competitive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You&#39;ve owned DevOps end-to-end: CI/CD pipelines, IaC (Terraform/Terragrunt preferred), container orchestration (Kubernetes), and the deployment and release practices that let engineers ship with confidence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You have a serious security mindset: AuthN/AuthZ, network isolation, secrets management, zero-trust and you&#39;ve hardened infrastructure in production at a company where the stakes were high.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You&#39;ve driven developer productivity improvements that engineering teams actually noticed: internal platforms, toolchain automation, deployment speed, or developer experience initiatives that meaningfully changed how your team works.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You actively use AI tools in your own work and have driven structured adoption on your team, not just encouraged it in the abstract, but changed how work gets done.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You&#39;ve led distributed teams across time zones. You know how to keep remote engineers aligned, accountable, and energized without defaulting to unnecessary synchronous overhead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You thrive in ambiguity, move fast when the picture is incomplete, and adjust quickly when priorities shift. You&#39;ve done this before at a startup or high-growth company.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Payments or crypto domain knowledge is a plus. QA/testing infrastructure experience is a bonus. But intellectual curiosity and a fast ramp matter more than a specific background.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bay Area based and willing to travel regularly to SF to work with the team and clients in person.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
